    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Between 700 and 900 CE, the Hohokam people built a large settlement south of present‑day Cashion.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cashion%2C_Arizona"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "That Hohokam site, now called the Cashion Ruin, featured ball courts and platform mounds along regional trade routes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cashion%2C_Arizona"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "An irrigation canal from the Salt River once fed the Cashion settlement, supporting its agriculture and daily life.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cashion%2C_Arizona"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In the late 1800s, Jim Cashion raised cattle and later farmed ostriches, even sending hats made from their feathers to Chandler.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cashion%2C_Arizona"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "During the 1920s, Cashion was a regular stop on the Buckeye line of the Arizona Eastern Railway, with trains picking up mail daily.",
